1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,896
This is Abu Thar Al-Halawaji
reciting Du'a al-Iftitah,

2
00:00:06,896 --> 00:00:09,585
and an app following him word by word.

3
00:00:09,585 --> 00:00:12,512
The models in it were never
trained on his voice.

4
00:00:12,512 --> 00:00:16,556
Here is what the app did with two and
a half minutes of his recitation,

5
00:00:16,556 --> 00:00:18,295
from his first word.

6
00:00:18,533 --> 00:00:22,263
Along the way, he stops for six
seconds, someone talks to him,

7
00:00:22,263 --> 00:00:24,547
and later he goes back two lines.

8
00:00:24,547 --> 00:00:27,107
People reading along do all of these.

9
00:00:35,167 --> 00:00:37,868
First, how does a phone
hear anything at all?

10
00:00:37,868 --> 00:00:43,217
Its microphone measures how the air
pressure changes, 16,000 times a second.

11
00:00:43,217 --> 00:00:49,267
So the basmala reaches the app as about
75,000 numbers, and nothing else.

12
00:00:49,267 --> 00:00:54,923
A list of numbers is hard to read, so
both models first turn it into a picture.

13
00:00:54,923 --> 00:01:00,142
Every hundredth of a second, the app
measures how much energy the sound has,

14
00:01:00,142 --> 00:01:04,399
from low to high in 80 steps,
and draws it as one thin column,

15
00:01:04,399 --> 00:01:06,922
brighter where there's more.

16
00:01:06,922 --> 00:01:11,485
Side by side, the columns show
each sound as a shape of its own.

17
00:01:11,485 --> 00:01:14,192
The s is a faint haze reaching to the top,

18
00:01:14,192 --> 00:01:18,273
and a vowel he draws out is
a stack of bright stripes.

19
00:01:18,567 --> 00:01:24,351
Reading those pictures is what the two
models do, and both are neural networks.

20
00:01:24,351 --> 00:01:27,031
A network is a long chain of simple sums,

21
00:01:27,031 --> 00:01:32,273
with millions of adjustable numbers
that set how much each part counts.

22
00:01:32,273 --> 00:01:37,033
In training, it's shown recordings
together with what was said in them,

23
00:01:37,033 --> 00:01:42,700
and those numbers are nudged, a little
at a time, until what comes out matches.

24
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:44,713
The first model is Whisper,

25
00:01:44,713 --> 00:01:50,382
made by OpenAI and trained on
680,000 hours of speech in many languages.

26
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:52,828
Its first half listens to the picture.

27
00:01:52,828 --> 00:01:56,800
Its second half writes down what
was said, a few letters at a time.

28
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:02,567
The app uses a small version, with 74 million
numbers, so that it runs on a phone.

29
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:07,502
Out of the box it barely understands
recitation, so it was trained further,

30
00:02:07,502 --> 00:02:13,100
on over 100 hours of recited du'as, and
on synthetic voices of ordinary people.

31
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,859
On the phone, Whisper gets the
last six seconds, once a second,

32
00:02:16,859 --> 00:02:20,262
and that's how the app works
out which du'a this is.

33
00:02:20,262 --> 00:02:23,448
The second model listens for
letters, ten times a second,

34
00:02:23,448 --> 00:02:25,705
and that's how it follows the word.

35
00:02:25,705 --> 00:02:27,757
More on that one later.

36
00:02:28,567 --> 00:02:33,834
He opens with the basmala and the salawat,
and Whisper gets the words right.

37
00:02:33,834 --> 00:02:40,210
But 66 of the texts open with the basmala,
and 41 of them go on with the salawat.

38
00:02:40,210 --> 00:02:44,194
The app looks for what Whisper
wrote in all 522 texts,

39
00:02:44,194 --> 00:02:48,995
and each text where it fits takes
a share of the probability.

40
00:02:48,995 --> 00:02:54,175
After seventeen seconds, the
likeliest is Du'a Tawassul, at 7%.

41
00:02:55,717 --> 00:02:57,533
Allāhumma innī

42
00:02:57,533 --> 00:03:00,875
Three texts carry on with
these words, and between them,

43
00:03:00,875 --> 00:03:03,743
they now hold most of the probability.

44
00:03:04,083 --> 00:03:06,700
aftatiḥu th-thanā'a

45
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:08,742
Only one text has these words.

46
00:03:08,742 --> 00:03:15,490
Iftitah goes to 94%, past the 70% the
app waits for, and its name comes up.

47
00:03:21,767 --> 00:03:23,712
Now it has to keep up with him.

48
00:03:23,712 --> 00:03:28,051
Whisper's text arrives more than a
second late, with no timing inside it,

49
00:03:28,051 --> 00:03:30,432
so it can't say which word he's on.

50
00:03:30,432 --> 00:03:33,033
The highlight comes from the second model.

51
00:03:33,033 --> 00:03:35,004
It's Whisper's listening half,

52
00:03:35,004 --> 00:03:39,414
with the writing half taken
off and one small layer added.

53
00:03:39,414 --> 00:03:42,456
For every fiftieth of a
second of the picture,

54
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:48,346
that layer gives a probability for each
Arabic letter, and for no letter at all.

55
00:03:48,346 --> 00:03:53,724
It learned by copying a far bigger
model, with about 300 million numbers,

56
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:57,433
trained on recitation
and on ordinary voices.

57
00:03:57,433 --> 00:03:59,824
Because it never writes words, it's quick,

58
00:03:59,824 --> 00:04:03,084
and every letter comes with
the moment it was heard.

59
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:06,942
Every tenth of a second, it hears
the last two seconds again.

60
00:04:06,942 --> 00:04:11,533
Here are the letters it found, newest
on the left, the way Arabic is read.

61
00:04:11,533 --> 00:04:15,362
The app lines those letters up
against the text of Iftitah,

62
00:04:15,362 --> 00:04:18,193
and keeps a probability for every line.

63
00:04:18,193 --> 00:04:19,868
Carrying on costs nothing.

64
00:04:19,868 --> 00:04:24,433
Saying the line again, going back a few
lines, or skipping ahead are allowed,

65
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:28,943
but each costs a little, so it takes
clear letters to make them happen.

66
00:04:28,943 --> 00:04:30,104
In this recitation,

67
00:04:30,104 --> 00:04:35,898
the highlight typically reached a word about
a third of a second after he started it.

68
00:04:39,033 --> 00:04:41,127
Here he stops for six seconds.

69
00:04:41,127 --> 00:04:46,848
Silence brings no letters, so nothing moves
the highlight: it stays on his last word.

70
00:04:48,933 --> 00:04:53,843
When he starts the next line, it follows
him a little over half a second later.

71
00:05:07,567 --> 00:05:09,636
That was someone talking to him.

72
00:05:09,636 --> 00:05:13,919
The app keeps a place for not reading,
and talk is meant to end up there.

73
00:05:13,919 --> 00:05:15,835
It didn't go there straight away:

74
00:05:15,835 --> 00:05:19,085
part of the talk sounded like
him starting line 9 again,

75
00:05:19,085 --> 00:05:22,578
and the highlight jumped back
to the start of the line.

76
00:05:22,578 --> 00:05:27,967
Then more of it went to not reading, and the
highlight waited there until he came back.

77
00:05:27,967 --> 00:05:31,506
He comes back with line 10,
and the highlight follows him.

78
00:05:31,506 --> 00:05:34,946
But Whisper's last six seconds
were still mostly talk,

79
00:05:34,946 --> 00:05:39,214
so for a few seconds the app doubted
it was hearing Iftitah at all:

80
00:05:39,214 --> 00:05:41,744
it might be a du'a it doesn't have.

81
00:05:41,744 --> 00:05:44,283
The letters kept the highlight on line 10,

82
00:05:44,283 --> 00:05:48,934
and once Whisper's window held his
words again, the doubt was gone.

83
00:05:58,767 --> 00:06:01,819
At the end of line 12,
he goes back to line 10.

84
00:06:01,819 --> 00:06:05,344
For three tenths of a second,
the likeliest line was 13,

85
00:06:05,344 --> 00:06:07,658
the one that usually comes next.

86
00:06:07,658 --> 00:06:12,196
But the highlight moves into a new line
only once it hears that line's words,

87
00:06:12,196 --> 00:06:14,028
so it stayed where it was.

88
00:06:14,028 --> 00:06:17,700
Then the letters of line 10 came
in, and it followed him back.

89
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,216
From there, he reads on to line 16.

90
00:06:20,216 --> 00:06:21,744
Over the whole recitation,

91
00:06:21,744 --> 00:06:27,104
the phone's highlight was on the word he
was saying about two thirds of the time.

92
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:29,636
That's one reading.

93
00:06:29,636 --> 00:06:34,244
On 22 hours of recordings from
55 voices the models never trained on,

94
00:06:34,244 --> 00:06:38,184
the du'a was named within
10 seconds nine times out of ten,

95
00:06:38,184 --> 00:06:42,358
and the highlight was on the
reader's line about as often.

96
00:06:42,358 --> 00:06:46,471
It does worst in echoing halls,
with the phone far from the reader,

97
00:06:46,471 --> 00:06:49,165
and when someone jumps around the du'a.

98
00:06:49,165 --> 00:06:52,177
And when someone recites a
du'a the app doesn't have,

99
00:06:52,177 --> 00:06:55,278
it shows some other one about half the time.
